My hat shades my face as I lay atop a tall-ish rock hill, a favorite. Just high enough to see past the grass-ridden valley I occupy. Vultures scouring the sky. Listening to a song a kind-figure once gifted me, it brings me back to earlier successes, that or unfounded feelings of being on a path that would lead me somewhere.

The southwest monsoon has taken on a special meaning for me. The clouds I imagine are like mountains in the sky, so significant that if you were to somehow stand at the base of one there would be an unmanageable sensation of magnitude. 

They swell and pour like clockwork everyday. Rain so heavy that you see rays of grey connecting sky and ground, beautifully spaced apart from one cloud in the desert landscape to the next. 

It’s this feeling of grandness that reminds me how big this world is.